-- S-structured-carry (#1937), ACCEPT direction: the same STRUCTURED declared
-- context, at a call site where the goal IS satisfiable.
--
-- Hand-derived from DICT-SEMANTICS §1/§4 BEFORE running anything: at
-- `f (Wrap 5) True` the instantiation is `a := Int, b := Bool`, so the goal is
-- `Conv (Wrap Int) Bool` -- exactly the impl in scope.  All three verbs must
-- ACCEPT and both engines must print `wrap-int-bool`.
--
-- 🚨 THE VALUE IS NOT WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR.  Base (4b45e035) already printed
-- `wrap-int-bool` at exit 0 here, so a verdict+value row alone grades green on
-- both sides of the fix and pins nothing.  What moved is the SCHEME, which
-- Section 2 asserts:
--
--     base: f : Wrap Int -> Bool -> String                (context ERASED)
--     now:  f : Conv (Wrap a) b => Wrap a -> b -> String  (context KEPT)
--
-- This is #607's and #610's discriminating shape one scope over -- the right
-- value printed with the constraint silently dropped from the type -- and the
-- scheme is the only observable that separates them.
--
-- The scheme is the declared `Wrap a -> b`.  Module-end head-tycon grounding
-- used to bind the signature's `a := Int, b := Bool` through the one impl in
-- scope and print `Wrap Int -> Bool` (the #3526 narrowing); a signature
-- variable is rigid in that matcher too (DICT-SEMANTICS §3, "Signature
-- variables in every matcher").
--
-- The pinned scheme text renders the predicate's structured argument in
-- parentheses -- `Conv (Wrap a) b` -- so the printed context round-trips
-- through the parser (#1952, landed).  Before #1952 the same predicate printed
-- as `Conv Wrap a b`, which reads as three arguments to a two-parameter
-- interface.  The s-nary-given-declared-vector-selects row pins the same scheme
-- with two impls in scope; this row's claim is that the declared context
-- survives at all.
data Wrap a = Wrap a

interface Conv a b where
  conv : a -> b -> String

impl Conv (Wrap Int) Bool where
  conv _ _ = "wrap-int-bool"

f : Conv (Wrap a) b => Wrap a -> b -> String
f x y = conv x y

main = println (f (Wrap 5) True)
